The Littlewood conjecture (and its p-adic variant) is a famous open problem in Diophantine approximation. It states that for any two real numbers α and β,

$$\liminf_{n\to\infty} n\,\|n\alpha\|\,\|n\beta\| = 0,$$

where $\|x\|$ denotes the distance to the nearest integer. This conjecture has remained open since it was posed by John Edensor Littlewood around 1930.

What is known:
- The conjecture has been proved for pairs $(\alpha,\beta)$ where at least one of the two numbers has unbounded partial quotients in its continued fraction expansion (this covers a full-measure set of pairs, by the theorem of Bernik–Kleinbock–Margulis and related work).
- Einsiedler, Katok, and Lindenstrauss (2006) showed that the set of exceptions (if any exist) has Hausdorff dimension zero, using measure rigidity of higher-rank diagonal actions on homogeneous spaces.
- The p-adic Littlewood conjecture is similarly open, with partial results (e.g., it holds when α has unbounded partial quotients; de Mathan and Teulié conjectured a related mixed-archimedean/p-adic statement).

However, a complete proof for arbitrary real $\alpha,\beta$ is not known, and no proof of the general statement is available. As an AI, I cannot produce a proof of a major open problem that the mathematical community has been unable to resolve for nearly a century.

